<section class="process-section section"><div class="wrap process-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">A controlled approach</p><h2>Our mold remediation process</h2><p>The exact scope depends on the property, but a responsible process follows the same priorities: stop moisture, limit spread, address affected materials and confirm the area is ready for the next stage.</p></div><ol class="steps"><li><span>1</span><div><h3>Assessment and moisture review</h3><p>Document visible conditions, water history, affected materials and likely moisture pathways.</p></div></li><li><span>2</span><div><h3>Work area planning</h3><p>Define access, containment, material handling and protection for adjacent areas.</p></div></li><li><span>3</span><div><h3>Removal and detailed cleaning</h3><p>Remove materials that cannot be retained and clean remaining surfaces using methods suited to the material.</p></div></li><li><span>4</span><div><h3>Drying and repair readiness</h3><p>Confirm source correction and drying needs before reconstruction covers the affected assembly.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>
<section class="image-content"><img src="/image/remove-mold.webp" alt="Mold remediation work on an affected interior wall" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><div class="image-copy"><p class="eyebrow dark">Work that fits the building</p><h2>Containment helps protect unaffected areas</h2><p>Disturbing mold affected drywall, insulation or other porous material can release dust and debris. Containment, controlled access and an appropriate cleaning sequence help keep the project organized.</p><p>The work plan should account for the size of the affected area, occupied rooms, pathways through the property and how removed materials will leave the site.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Control access to the work zone</li><li>Protect nearby finishes and contents</li><li>Remove affected material carefully</li><li>Clean before containment is removed</li></ul></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap content-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow dark">Common locations</p><h2>Where mold is found in West Vancouver properties</h2></div><div class="prose"><h3>Attics and roof spaces</h3><p>Roof leaks, bathroom exhaust, ceiling air leakage and seasonal condensation can create dark staining on wood sheathing. The moisture pattern must be understood before treatment.</p><h3>Basements and crawl spaces</h3><p>Foundation moisture, drainage, exposed soil and plumbing leaks can keep framing and stored materials damp. Below grade conditions often require water management as well as remediation.</p><h3>Exterior walls and windows</h3><p>Condensation, failed seals, envelope leaks and limited air circulation behind furniture can produce recurring growth near colder surfaces.</p><h3>Bathrooms, kitchens and laundry rooms</h3><p>Plumbing, exhaust, appliance connections and repeated humidity make these rooms common starting points for moisture investigations.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="area-section section" id="service-areas"><div class="wrap area-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow dark">Local service coverage</p><h2>Serving West Vancouver neighbourhoods</h2><p>Service is focused on West Vancouver homes, condos, strata properties and commercial spaces. Local climate, site drainage, building age and construction details are considered as part of the assessment.</p><p>Call <a href="tel:+16048003900"><strong>(604) 800-3900</strong></a> or use the request form to explain what you found.</p></div><ul class="areas"><li>Ambleside</li><li>Dundarave</li><li>British Properties</li><li>Caulfeild</li><li>Horseshoe Bay</li><li>Eagle Harbour</li><li>Gleneagles</li><li>West Bay</li><li>Sentinel Hill</li><li>Upper Levels</li></ul></div></section>
